1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:16,440
A wave is a deflection; a complex is a group of deflections. A segment
lies between defined events, while an interval includes one or more
events and the time between boundaries. These words tell you where to
place your measurement endpoints.

2
00:00:16,440 --> 00:00:34,032
For example, the PR interval starts at the beginning of the P wave,
not its end. The QT interval begins with ventricular depolarization,
not with the P wave. If the endpoints are wrong, accurate box counting
still produces the wrong physiological measurement.

3
00:00:34,032 --> 00:00:53,400
The P wave represents atrial depolarization. The school uses duration
below zero point one two seconds and amplitude below two point five
millimetres as introductory references, with lead-dependent
appearance. An unusual P wave needs context; shape alone does not
diagnose the cause.

4
00:00:53,400 --> 00:01:14,136
PR measures from P onset to QRS onset, normally about zero point one
two to zero point two zero seconds in this teaching framework. It
includes atrial and AV conduction, not just AV-node delay. Compare
successive conducted beats and look for P waves that fail to produce a
QRS.

5
00:01:14,136 --> 00:01:34,056
Measure QRS width from the first ventricular deflection through its
final return to the ST segment. A width below zero point one two
seconds is the school reference for a narrow complex. A wide complex
suggests slower or altered activation, not automatically one specific
rhythm.

6
00:01:34,056 --> 00:01:54,816
Bundle-branch conduction, ventricular origin, metabolic disturbance
and pacing can all alter QRS appearance. Low amplitude also has
technical and patient-related explanations. Do not equate a larger
complex with stronger contraction, or a narrow complex with adequate
perfusion.

7
00:01:54,816 --> 00:02:17,520
Identify the J point where QRS ends and ST begins. Assess ST
displacement relative to an appropriate isoelectric reference, not the
peak or end of the T wave. ST elevation, depression or T-wave
inversion can be important, but a single monitoring lead cannot
complete diagnostic twelve-lead interpretation.

8
00:02:17,520 --> 00:02:39,024
A peaked, flattened or inverted T wave raises possibilities such as
electrolyte disturbance, ischemia or altered activation. It does not
establish a laboratory potassium concentration or a coronary
diagnosis. Repeat a technically sound tracing, compare appropriate
leads and integrate symptoms and physiology.

9
00:02:39,024 --> 00:02:59,928
QT extends from QRS onset to the end of the T wave. It includes
ventricular depolarization and repolarization. Because it changes with
heart rate, a raw duration cannot be interpreted without rate
information. The later QTc chapter supplies a measured, rate-adjusted
approach.

10
00:02:59,928 --> 00:03:17,136
The school mentions a quick comparison with half the preceding RR
interval. Treat that only as a rough screening idea, not a reliable
normality rule at all rates. Distinguish a separate U wave and
document unclear T-wave endpoints rather than assigning false
precision.

11
00:03:17,136 --> 00:03:40,224
When the school asks you to distinguish atrial from ventricular
events, explain rather than merely sort labels. P is atrial
activation. PR spans transmission from atrial activation to
ventricular activation. QRS, ST and T describe ventricular activation,
plateau-related behaviour and recovery.

12
00:03:40,224 --> 00:03:57,984
Finish every interpretation with its limits. A normal-looking waveform
may occur early in disease, while an abnormal waveform may result from
artifact or placement. Describe what you observed, state what remains
uncertain, and check whether the patient is deteriorating.
